 "sources": "Fernando José Wolf and Conrado Hofmann, <i lang=\"es\">Primavera y flor de romances</i>, vol. 2 (Berlin, A. Asher y Comp., 1856), for the ballad; Jorge Manrique, <i lang=\"es\">Poesías</i> (Barcelona, Antonio López, Colección Diamante, vol. 114), for the first stanza of the <i lang=\"es\">Coplas</i>. The lines are given as those editions print them. The prologue of the Manrique volume says that the edition adopts modern orthography and keeps an old form only when the line or the rhyme requires it. Garcilaso de la Vega, <i lang=\"es\">Obras</i>, ilustradas con notas, second edition (Madrid, Imprenta de Sancha, 1817), for Sonnet XXIII. Gustavo Adolfo Bécquer, <i lang=\"es\">Rimas</i>, novísima edición americana (San Francisco, The History Company, 1891), for the first stanza of Rima LIII. The editors’ preface says that the text was collated with the original. Luis de Góngora, <i lang=\"es\">Poesías</i> (Madrid, 1820), for the opening of the letrilla that begins <i lang=\"es\">Ande yo caliente</i>. San Juan de la Cruz, <i lang=\"es\">Obras</i>, tomo tercero (Toledo, Viuda e Hijos de J. Peláez, 1914), edited by Padre Gerardo de San Juan de la Cruz, for the first two stanzas of the poem that begins <i lang=\"es\">En una noche oscura</i>. Federico García Lorca, <i lang=\"es\">Primer romancero gitano</i> (Madrid, Revista de Occidente, 1928), for the opening of “Romance de la luna, luna.” José de Espronceda, <i lang=\"es\">Poesías</i> (Madrid, Imprenta de Yenes, 1840), for the opening of the song that begins <i lang=\"es\">Con diez cañones por banda</i> and for the refrain that begins <i lang=\"es\">Que es mi barco mi tesoro</i>. Francisco de Quevedo, <i lang=\"es\">Obras</i>, poesías, tomo tercero (Madrid, M. Rivadeneyra, 1877), collected by Florencio Janer, for the sonnet that begins <i lang=\"es\">Cerrar podrá mis ojos</i>. Rubén Darío, <i lang=\"es\">Prosas profanas y otros poemas</i> (Buenos Aires, Imprenta de Pablo E. Coni é Hijos, 1896), for the first stanza of “Sonatina.” Felipe Robles Dégano, <i lang=\"es\">Ortología clásica de la lengua castellana</i> (1905), for the count of syllables and for the old practice in assonance. The English of the verse is the course’s own plain rendering, line by line. The prologue of the Manrique volume states that it modernizes the spelling. The editions of Góngora (1820) and of San Juan (1914) print modern spelling. The edition of Garcilaso (1817) keeps <i lang=\"es\">ayrado</i> and <i lang=\"es\">elado</i>. The edition of Quevedo (Janer, 1877) keeps <i lang=\"es\">x</i> in <i lang=\"es\">dexará</i> and <i lang=\"es\">dexarán</i>. The edition of Wolf and Hofmann states its policy in the introduction to its first volume (page VII): <i lang=\"es\">hemos adoptado la ortografía, puntuación y acentuación según ahora se usan, conservando solamente la ortografía de los originales cuando señala al mismo tiempo una diferencia etimológica</i>, that is, the editors adopted the spelling, punctuation, and accentuation of their own day and kept the spelling of the originals only where it marks an etymological difference. So <i lang=\"es\">yago</i> is kept as an older form, and <i lang=\"es\">dia</i> and <i lang=\"es\">galardon</i> are written without the accent that today’s spelling writes. All these poems are quoted as printed. The counts of syllables follow the rules of the meter card and Robles Dégano.",
