   "id": "poetry-18",
   "title": "Eleven syllables, rose and lily",
   "body": "<p>Each line of the sonnet is a hendecasyllable, a line of eleven syllables. The line ends on a word stressed on the next to last syllable, so nothing is added and nothing is taken away.</p><p><i lang=\"es\">En tanto que de rosa y azucena</i> is counted here. <i lang=\"es\">En</i>, <i lang=\"es\">tan</i>, <i lang=\"es\">to</i>, <i lang=\"es\">que</i>, <i lang=\"es\">de</i>, <i lang=\"es\">ro</i>, <i lang=\"es\">sa</i>, then the <i lang=\"es\">y</i> with the first vowel of <i lang=\"es\">azucena</i>, then <i lang=\"es\">zu</i>, <i lang=\"es\">ce</i>, <i lang=\"es\">na</i>. That is eleven. The stress of <i lang=\"es\">rosa</i> falls on <i lang=\"es\">ro</i>, the sixth syllable, and the stress of <i lang=\"es\">azucena</i> falls on <i lang=\"es\">ce</i>, the tenth. Felipe Robles Dégano says (<i lang=\"es\">Ortología clásica</i>, §99, page 86) that a conjunction between two vowels almost always joins the second vowel and cannot join the vowel before it and the vowel after it at once, and his examples include <i lang=\"es\">y</i>. If the <i lang=\"es\">y</i> joined <i lang=\"es\">rosa</i> instead, the line would still have eleven syllables.</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Se muestra la color en vuestro gesto</i> has no synalepha. <i lang=\"es\">Se</i>, <i lang=\"es\">mues</i>, <i lang=\"es\">tra</i>, <i lang=\"es\">la</i>, <i lang=\"es\">co</i>, <i lang=\"es\">lor</i>, <i lang=\"es\">en</i>, <i lang=\"es\">vues</i>, <i lang=\"es\">tro</i>, <i lang=\"es\">ges</i>, <i lang=\"es\">to</i>. The vowels <i lang=\"es\">ue</i> in <i lang=\"es\">muestra</i> and in <i lang=\"es\">vuestro</i> are a diphthong, one syllable. <i lang=\"es\">Color</i> is stressed on <i lang=\"es\">lor</i>, the sixth syllable. <i lang=\"es\">Gesto</i> is stressed on <i lang=\"es\">ges</i>, the tenth.</p>"
