   "body": "<p><i lang=\"es\">Coged de vuestra alegre primavera el dulce fruto.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Coged</i> is the imperative of the second person plural, from <i lang=\"es\">coger</i>. The sonnet speaks to one lady, but it addresses her with the respectful <i lang=\"es\">vos</i>, which takes the plural forms of the verb, as <i lang=\"es\">vuestro gesto</i> and <i lang=\"es\">vuestra alegre primavera</i> show. <i lang=\"es\">Primavera</i> is the spring, and <i lang=\"es\">fruto</i> is the fruit of it. <i lang=\"es\">Antes que el tiempo ayrado cubra de nieve la hermosa cumbre.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Antes que</i> is “before.” <i lang=\"es\">Ayrado</i> agrees with <i lang=\"es\">tiempo</i>. <i lang=\"es\">Cubra</i> is the present subjunctive of <i lang=\"es\">cubrir</i>, after <i lang=\"es\">antes que</i>. <i lang=\"es\">Cumbre</i> is the summit.</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Marchitará la rosa el viento elado.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Marchitará</i> is the future of <i lang=\"es\">marchitar</i>, “will wither.” The subject is <i lang=\"es\">el viento elado</i>, the frozen wind, and <i lang=\"es\">elado</i> agrees with <i lang=\"es\">viento</i>. <i lang=\"es\">Todo lo mudará la edad ligera.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Mudará</i> is the future of <i lang=\"es\">mudar</i>, “will change,” and the subject is <i lang=\"es\">la edad ligera</i>, the swift age. <i lang=\"es\">Por no hacer mudanza en su costumbre</i>: <i lang=\"es\">mudanza</i> is “change,” and <i lang=\"es\">costumbre</i> is “custom.” The age changes everything else, and its own custom is not to change.</p>"
