   "title": "Quevedo, Love constant beyond death · Hendecasyllable",
   "body": "<p lang=\"es\">Cerrar podrá mis ojos la postrera<br>Sombra, que me llevare el blanco dia;<br>Y podrá desatar esta alma mia<br>Hora , á su afan ansioso lisonjera ;<br>Mas no de esotra parte en la ribera<br>Dexará la memoria en donde ardia ;<br>Nadar sabe mi llama la agua fria,<br>Y perder el respeto á ley severa;<br>Alma, á quien todo un Dios prisión ha sido,<br>Venas, que humor á tanto fuego han dado,<br>Médulas, que han gloriosamente ardido,<br>Su cuerpo dexarán, no su cuidado ;<br>Serán ceniza, mas tendrá sentido,<br>Polvo serán, mas polvo enamorado.</p><p>The last shadow will be able to close my eyes,<br>the shadow that will take the white day from me;<br>and an hour will be able to untie this soul of mine,<br>an hour flattering to its anxious longing;<br>but it will not, on that other side, on the shore,<br>leave the memory where it burned;<br>my flame knows how to swim the cold water,<br>and to lose respect for a severe law.<br>A soul, for whom a whole God has been a prison,<br>veins, which have given fluid to so much fire,<br>marrows, which have burned gloriously,<br>will leave their body, not their care;<br>they will be ash, but it will have feeling,<br>they will be dust, but dust in love.</p><p><strong>Context.</strong> Francisco de Quevedo, of Madrid, died at Villanueva de los Infantes in 1645. This sonnet is taken from his poems in the Madrid volume of 1877, collected by Florencio Janer. The print heads it <i lang=\"es\">Amor constante más allá de la muerte</i>. The line is the hendecasyllable, the line of eleven syllables. The beloved is not named in these lines. What remains after the body is the care, and the dust is still in love.</p>",
