   "title": "Eleven syllables",
   "body": "<p><i lang=\"es\">Cerrar podrá mis ojos la postrera.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Ce</i>, <i lang=\"es\">rrar</i>, <i lang=\"es\">po</i>, <i lang=\"es\">drá</i>, <i lang=\"es\">mis</i>, <i lang=\"es\">o</i>, <i lang=\"es\">jos</i>, <i lang=\"es\">la</i>, <i lang=\"es\">pos</i>, <i lang=\"es\">tre</i>, <i lang=\"es\">ra</i>. Eleven. No two vowels meet. <i lang=\"es\">Ojos</i> is stressed on <i lang=\"es\">o</i>, the sixth syllable. <i lang=\"es\">Postrera</i> is stressed on <i lang=\"es\">tre</i>, the tenth.</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Sombra, que me llevare el blanco dia.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Dia</i> is two syllables, <i lang=\"es\">di</i> and <i lang=\"es\">a</i>, and the print writes no accent on it. <i lang=\"es\">Llevare</i> joins <i lang=\"es\">el</i>. The stress of <i lang=\"es\">llevare</i> falls on <i lang=\"es\">va</i>, and the stress of <i lang=\"es\">dia</i> falls on <i lang=\"es\">di</i>. If <i lang=\"es\">dia</i> were one syllable, the line would have ten. A stressed weak vowel beside another vowel makes two syllables, as in <i lang=\"es\">tra-ído</i> and <i lang=\"es\">re-úne</i> (Robles Dégano, <i>Ortología clásica</i>, §9). The count of eleven on this line rests on that division.</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Polvo serán, mas polvo enamorado.</i> <i lang=\"es\">Pol</i>, <i lang=\"es\">vo</i>, <i lang=\"es\">se</i>, <i lang=\"es\">rán</i>, <i lang=\"es\">mas</i>, <i lang=\"es\">pol</i>, <i lang=\"es\">voe</i>, <i lang=\"es\">na</i>, <i lang=\"es\">mo</i>, <i lang=\"es\">ra</i>, <i lang=\"es\">do</i>. The <i lang=\"es\">o</i> of the second <i lang=\"es\">polvo</i> joins <i lang=\"es\">e</i>. Eleven. <i lang=\"es\">Polvo</i> is stressed on <i lang=\"es\">pol</i>, the sixth, and <i lang=\"es\">enamorado</i> on <i lang=\"es\">ra</i>, the tenth.</p>"
